                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Combines tensors according to a short formula naming their dimensions. Each input gets a letter for each of its dimensions; letters shared between inputs are multiplied together, and letters missing from the result (after ->) are summed away.\n\nij,jk->ik     matrix product (t.matmul)\nij,ij->i      the dot product of each row of a with the same row of b\nij->j         the sum down each column\nii->          the trace: the sum of the diagonal\nbij,bjk->bik  a stack of matrix products\ni,j->ij       the outer product: every a times every b\n\nIt says exactly which dimensions meet, so it is clear and fast where t.matmul would need t.permute and t.unsqueeze around it.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.einsum <formula>\nDefault: ij,jk->ik. The number of commas before -> sets the number of inlets; the\nformula can be changed later in the subscripts option, with the same number of inputs.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe first tensor. Every input computes and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2, in 3, ...:\nThe other tensors, held, converted to in's device and type.\n\nsubscripts:\nAn option, under the node: the formula.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe tensor the formula describes.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nij,ij->i: three rows of a, each multiplied by the same row of b and summed - three dot products at once. 1 2 . 1 0 = 1; 3 4 . 0 1 = 4; 5 6 . 1 1 = 11.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.einsum is the numpy version. t.matmul is the matrix product. t.sum sums along one dimension."
        },
